			<div class="content" id="post-content-3583462">
									

<p>This is a <em>very</em> old thread, but since I'm downvoting for the same reason I think it's worth concluding.</p>
<p>Most of these seemingly ridiculous As Seen On TV products are designed and marketed for people with serious physical disabilities. To try to improve the marketing appeal, able-bodied actors use exagerated clumsiness to mimic the effects of those disabilities in a way that seems goofy to the average viewer, but instantly recognizable to the target audience. It's an ill-conceived dog whistle of sorts.</p>
<p>The article doesn't seem aware of that subtext, and while it doesn't show any ill intent, it's still well into uncomfortable territory.</p>

