					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/petrograd" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(455145); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=455145&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606769085" alt="Petrograd" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=455145)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/petrograd" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(455145); return false;" >Petrograd</a></span> <span class="odate time_1433959627 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">10 Jun 2015 18:07</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2314676">
									

<p>I think this is a case of ambiguousness harming an article.</p>
<p>So, there are three elements that I see. First, it's an uncontained, poorly understood, and contagious SCP. That's a stylistic choice that takes some amount of effort and intentional restraint to maintain. Here, I think it lacks a bit of a sense of urgency and makes the content a little less punchy than it might be.</p>
<p>Second, you're extracting horror from infomercial behavior. I think you're fairly successful there despite the cheesy roots of the idea, so that worked out.</p>
<p>Then you have by far the most interesting element to me, the mysterious facilities. There are a bunch of possibilities for this.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">just&nbsp;a&nbsp;few</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>From least to most interesting, for example, you could have:<br />
1) Foundation incompetence or unwieldiness<br />
2) Some kind of interdimensional crossover<br />
3) Someone impersonating the Foundation and abandoning their warehouses for&#8230; reasons<br />
4) The Foundation having created SCP-2407 for some reason<br />
5) The former containment of 2407 having gone horribly wrong, somehow leading to loss of records and the current state of 2407</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<br />
Unfortunately, you don't give any indication which of these ideas might be plausible. There's no evidence to support or even suggest any particular theory. Instead of this allowing me to use my imagination to fill in the gap, this honestly just doesn't give me anything to sink my teeth into. The final result is that I just don't care, which is sad for such an interesting gambit.
<p>Ultimately, no vote.</p>

							</div>

								
					
